{"ts":"2026-10-10T11:29:28.907Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"reasoning","reasoning":"I should check evaluate.ts around the name check logic and the existing guardrail tests. I'll time-box this to about an hour, starting with a plan for the admin passcode: a pure function that checks an expected passcode against a provided one and returns whether access is open, ok, or denied.\n\nSince timing-safe comparison needs node:crypto, I'll move that logic into a server-side adminGuard function that hashes with sha256 and uses timingSafeEqual, applying it across all four admin routes. I also need to rethink the admin page's server-side rendering of history — if passcode protection is on, I shouldn't leak history via SSR, so I'll pass an empty initial history and let the client fetch after the passcode is entered, storing the passcode client-side in sessionStorage.\n\nFor validating the passcode client-side, I can reuse the existing GET /api/admin/rules endpoint rather than adding a new verify route — a 200 means unlocked, 401 means wrong passcode. For testing, I need to verify the guard runs and rejects before any dynamic `connection()` call executes, and confirm process.env reads work correctly when tests set ADMIN_PASSCODE, while also considering whether Next's prerendering could interfere with the GET route under cacheComponents.\n\nThere's a real risk that at build time, if ADMIN_PASSCODE happens to be set, Next could prerender the GET route and bake in a static 401 response since the guard check might run before `connection()` makes it dynamic — so I should call `connection()` first and move the guard logic into the handler functions themselves (like `handleList`), with routes just awaiting `connection()` then delegating. That way tests can call the handlers directly, and POST routes aren't prerendered anyway so testing through the route layer works fine for those. I'm still uncertain whether simply reading `req.headers` in a GET route is enough to mark it dynamic under Next 16's cacheComponents behavior.\n\nFor the guardrails audit, I'm mapping out how Aadhaar masking should be verified across the UI (ConfirmStep only showing last4, schema rejecting longer digit strings), logs (extending the existing log-sanitization test to cover classifyRejection), and print output (testing that CoverSheet's maskAadhaar utility exists and possibly rendering CoverSheet via react-dom/server in a node test environment).\n\nI'm now double-checking whether Vitest's esbuild setup will correctly handle JSX in a .tsx component imported from a .ts test file, since Next's tsconfig jsx setting might differ from Vite's default automatic transform — rather than reasoning it out further, I decide it's simpler to just try rendering CoverSheet/CheckItem/ReportStep directly and see if it works.\n\nIf it does, I can build guardrail tests confirming the disclaimer appears on reports and cover sheets, every check shows \"verified on\" with a provisional badge, and Aadhaar numbers are masked with no account numbers shown in print. I'm also planning a source-level audit test that scans everything under src/ to confirm no stray `writeFile`, `createWriteStream`, or `localStorage` calls exist outside store.ts, and that the schema itself has no image column, reinforcing the no-images-stored guarantee.\n\nI need to allow sessionStorage specifically for the admin passcode as an exception in that audit test. I'm also relying on zod validation throughout — extractDocument already uses RawExtraction.safeParse, and I'll extend that pattern to classifyRejection and agent tools via checked(), with a fallback test for malformed AI output. Finally, I want property-based tests confirming every report always includes the disclaimer and every check always carries provenance, verification timestamp, and provisional badge fields.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the pure decoder module: defining a RawClassification schema with a rejection code and confidence score, then a decodeResult function that maps that into a status of matched or needs-verification, along with fix steps and bilingual office questions based on a confidence threshold.\n\nI'm setting DECODE_MIN_CONFIDENCE to 0.7 as the cutoff — below that or when the code is UNKNOWN, the result becomes needs_verification. In that case I still surface the guessed code as a \"possible\" reason (unless it's UNKNOWN), fall back to generic UNKNOWN fix steps, and attach a fixed bilingual (English/Assamese) list of questions to ask at the office.\n\nNow I'm designing classifyRejection, which calls a chat completion with the rejection codes/descriptions at temperature 0, validates the response with zod, retries once on failure, and logs with an input hash while capping text at 2000 characters. The POST /api/decode route validates the incoming text, invokes classifyRejection, and if the AI call fails or is unavailable, it still returns a 200 response with a needs_verification result (UNKNOWN) rather than erroring out.\n\nFor the /decode page, I'm planning a server page wrapping a client DecodeApp component with language toggle, textarea, and a \"Try an example\" sample button for demo purposes. The result card should show the title and fix steps primarily in the selected language, with the other language shown below in smaller text to keep things simple while still satisfying the \"Assamese + English\" requirement.\n\nI need to add decode-related strings to i18n.ts, keeping both languages in sync since the test enforces matching keys, and add a home page link (\"Got a rejection? Decode it\") pointing users to /decode. For tests, I'll cover the core decoder logic, the AI classifier with mocked fetch, and the API handler with a mocked ai module, then move on to writing the demo-reset Node script.\n\nSince only the store module should touch the database, I'm weighing whether a plain .mjs script can reset data without importing TS directly, and whether Node's native type-stripping (available by default since Node 23.6+) would let me run a .ts script instead, given that type-only imports get erased cleanly.\n\nI think a simpler, rule-compliant approach is better: add a `clearAll()` method to RuleStore that deletes all rule versions in a transaction, and write a test for it in store.test.ts, rather than relying on an ad-hoc reset script with uncertain Node type-stripping behavior.\n\nActually, switching the script to a `.mjs` file avoids the tsc issue entirely since type checking skips it, while still letting Node import the `.ts` store module directly via type stripping.\n\nI also realize deleting rule_versions rows only affects a fresh server start — a running server's in-memory registry keeps published versions since syncRegistry only adds, never removes. I need to either document restarting the server after demo:reset, or add an unregister capability so syncRegistry can also drop versions no longer in the database.\n\nAdding a resetRegistry export that clears the registry and re-registers the built-in schemes seems worth the small effort since it's needed for the demo reset flow — the server will pick up fresh data automatically. I'll verify with a test that publishes, clears, and resyncs the registry to confirm versions revert correctly, plus a manual check against the real database.\n\nFor polish I'll mostly skip it aside from maybe a loading state on decode, and treat the age-limit feature as optional depending on time — I should check how AgeRange and its max value are actually used before deciding. I also want to note the future scope in the progress file, then briefly review the evaluate logic, existing fuzzy-match tests, the store and rules tests, plus the tsconfig and eslint setup.\n\n"}}
