{"ts":"2026-10-10T11:30:49.666Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"reasoning","reasoning":"I'll skip middleware and handle the passcode check directly in the handlers for simplicity and testability. I'm planning to put a pure `checkAdminPasscode` function in a core auth module that takes the header value and expected passcode as arguments, keeping environment variable access out of the core logic.\n\nFor the server side, I'll add a `requireAdmin` helper that each protected route calls, and I'm thinking through how the admin page should behave when a passcode is set: the server shouldn't render history data without verification, so the page should pass an empty initial history and a \"protected\" flag, letting the client prompt for the passcode, store it in session storage, and retry the fetch with the header attached, clearing it on a 401.\n\nFor the actual comparison, I'll hash both the provided and expected passcodes and compare them with a constant-time equality check, returning a simple unauthorized error response on failure.\n\nOn the client, the AdminApp component needs a protected-mode flag and some passcode state to manage the prompt flow.\n\nI'm now considering how to persist the passcode in sessionStorage without triggering hydration issues or violating the new eslint rule against setting state inside effects — thinking useSyncExternalStore might be the cleaner solution here since it avoids that setState-in-effect pattern entirely.\n\nFor protected routes, any request returning 401 should clear sessionStorage and re-lock with a message, since run() and decide() also send the auth header. I also need an unprotected-mode banner notifying that passcode isn't set, plus tests covering requireAdmin behavior (unset env, missing/wrong/right header, whitespace-only env) and route-level tests verifying unauthorized requests are rejected before reaching the agent logic while authorized ones succeed.\n\nI'll make requireAdmin accept an injectable env object so tests can set and clean up process.env.ADMIN_PASSCODE directly, and ensure the admin page server component skips sending history when protected. Then I'm moving to the guardrails audit — verifying Aadhaar is masked everywhere (UI confirm step, print cover sheet, and logs) and thinking about how to test the cover sheet's masking by rendering it to static markup.\n\nSince the component files are .tsx and Vitest's transform should handle JSX automatically, I plan to write a guardrails test that renders CoverSheet, ReportStep, and CheckItem via renderToStaticMarkup, confirming none of them rely on hooks that would complicate server-side rendering.\n\nI'm outlining specific checks: verifying the CoverSheet masks Aadhaar numbers correctly and never leaks bank account numbers, confirming the schema strictly rejects full Aadhaar numbers and unknown fields, making sure disclaimers appear in both English and Assamese across all report steps and sample schemes, and checking that every CheckItem shows its source title, verification date, and provisional status correctly.\n\nI'm also planning a static guard test that scans source files (excluding store.ts) to confirm no filesystem write calls, browser storage APIs, or direct sqlite imports exist outside the designated store module, and that the database schema has no image/blob columns — ensuring no uploaded documents are ever persisted as files or images.\n\nFor model-output validation, I'll rely on ai.test.ts covering extractDocument's fallback on malformed output plus new tests for classifyRejection rejecting bad codes or out-of-range confidence scores, and add a static scan confirming only ai.ts references chat/completions, the Agent constructor, or the API key — keeping model-call logic isolated from the rest of the codebase.\n\nFor fuzzy matching, I'll lean on the existing property test in evaluate.test.ts that guarantees it never silently approves, plus a cross-check across samples that any \"ok\" result always has a \"same\" match level.\n\nI should also verify Aadhaar numbers stay masked: ConfirmStep only shows the last 4 digits, and I want a test asserting no 12-digit sequences ever appear in report markup across samples, plus a check that classifyRejection logs never leak the pasted input text. I still need to check whether CoverSheet displays provenance like a \"verified on\" timestamp.\n\nLooking closer, CoverSheet only shows the provisional marker without source info, so I'll add the source title and verified-on date to each cover sheet line for completeness. The decode page should also get a disclaimer footer since it behaves like a report. For the decoder itself, I need to add new constants to rejectionCodes.ts.\n\nDefining the schema...\n\nIf code isn't UNKNOWN, I'll surface it as a \"possible\" match with a confirm-at-office note, keeping the fix shape simple with a nullable possible field. I'm drafting the bilingual office-question list carefully, double-checking the Assamese characters for accuracy, then moving to the classifyRejection function that calls the AI with a versioned prompt and returns either a code/confidence pair or an error.\n\nI'm validating the input length, adding a single retry with zod parsing for the classification shape, and logging a hash of the input text rather than raw content. I'm deciding against refactoring the existing fetch loop into a shared helper, keeping classify self-contained even with some duplication, and I'll extend the log entry union to include the new function name and an invalid_input outcome, plus include short code descriptions in the prompt itself.\n\nI also need to guard against prompt injection since the input text is untrusted — wrapping it in clear delimiters and instructing the model to ignore embedded instructions. For the API layer, I'm sketching a request schema with length constraints and a handler that calls the classifier, mapping failures to appropriate status codes, while considering whether to still surface a fallback UI listing office-ask options when AI is unavailable.\n\nI'm planning the route handler, the server page with metadata, and a client component with a language toggle defaulting to Assamese, a textarea, and example buttons like a sample Aadhaar payment failure message.\n\nI'm thinking through where to add the \"Decode it\" link on the home page, placing it below the scheme list on the scheme step, and planning the i18n string key for it.\n\nI'm outlining the test coverage needed: core decodeView tests for confidence thresholds and bilingual steps, plus AI classification tests with a mocked fetch returning a specific rejection code.\n\nI'm also mapping out the API handler tests for the decode endpoint, including success, error fallback, and bad request paths, then thinking about how to build the demo-reset script given that only the store module should touch the database, and considering whether Node's native TypeScript stripping support lets a .mjs script import the TS path aliases directly.\n\nI should switch to a .mjs script instead, since .mjs files fall outside the tsconfig include patterns and won't be type-checked, while still being able to dynamically import the .ts store module at runtime. I also need to consider module detection since the package has no \"type\" field — Node's newer syntax detection should still treat the .ts file as ESM correctly, though it might emit a warning.\n\nA cleaner approach is adding a `resetToBuiltins()` method to RuleStore that deletes all rule_versions in a transaction, then having the script simply instantiate the store, call that method, and log the result. I confirm type-stripping is enabled by default since Node v22.18/v23.6, so this should work without flags.\n\nBut I realize resetting the database alone doesn't fix a running server — its in-memory registry still holds stale published schemes until restart, since syncRegistry only adds and never removes. I should either add registry removal logic or have RuleService.syncRegistry detect non-builtin versions no longer present in the store and trigger a full registry reset plus re-registration of currently published schemes.\n\nRegistering missing versions on sync is cheap per request and doesn't need a server restart, since running servers pick it up on the next request. I should verify this with a test: publish a version, reset the store, sync, then check that getScheme returns the updated version and listSchemes shows the right count — and I'll only bother with the AgeRange max optional change if it's quick.\n\nFor docs, the standard Next.js patterns I'm using (headers, connection(), Link, client-side sessionStorage) should all be fine, and a static page with a client component should prerender without issue. I want to quickly check how DocsStep/ConfirmStep handle the Aadhaar input to make sure it's restricted to 4 digits.\n\nI've laid out a four-part plan covering admin passcode protection, a guardrails audit test, a rejection decoder feature, and a demo:reset script. Now I'll check a few more files before starting implementation.\n\n"}}
