{"ts":"2026-10-10T11:31:56.038Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"reasoning","reasoning":"I'm weighing whether to add a resetAll() method to RuleStore so only the store module touches the database, but I need to check if Node's type-stripping can handle the `@/` path aliases and type-only imports in store.ts without a build step.\n\nWithout a \"type\": \"module\" field, Node's syntax detection should still treat the .ts file's import statements correctly, but I should just test it to be sure. I also realize resetting the database won't clear a running server's in-memory registry, so I'll need to document that users should restart the server after running the reset script to see the home page list only the original schemes.\n\nActually simpler: an `unregisterScheme(id, version)` function works fine since builtins never enter the registered set, and each server process syncs independently per request.\n\nNow I'm outlining the implementation plan, starting with admin passcode handling — I'll add a timing-safe comparison function and a `requireAdmin` helper that checks the request against an environment-configured passcode.\n\nI need to apply this check across all four routes, but the GET list route is trickier since it's invoked both from a server component page (which shouldn't require the header) and from route handlers in tests. I'm deciding to place the auth check in the route files themselves, before the data connection step, so the page logic stays unaffected while route tests still exercise the check.\n\nLooking further, I realize the admin page currently server-renders the scheme history unconditionally, which would leak protected data even when a passcode is set. To stay consistent with the spec, I should make the page avoid server-rendering history when protection is enabled, instead showing a passcode box and letting the client fetch the data with the proper header.\n\nFor the client side, I need to read the stored passcode from sessionStorage after mount to avoid hydration mismatches, then trigger a fetch that unlocks the admin view if valid or shows an error if not.\n\nNext I want to add a visible \"Demo mode: unprotected\" amber banner when protection isn't enabled, then move into checking guardrails — confirming Aadhaar numbers stay masked across the UI, logs, and printed documents via the confirm step validation and the cover sheet's masking function.\n\nFor testing, I'll add a `src/guardrails.test.ts` covering: ExtractedDoc rejecting a full 12-digit aadhaar value, a static check that CoverSheet source references maskAadhaar and never raw account numbers, and verifying log entries exclude sensitive inputs (including the pasted text during rejection classification). I also want to confirm I can render React components in tests using react-dom, since no testing library is set up.\n\nI'll also verify no image storage is happening anywhere by scanning for file-write functions like writeFile or createWriteStream across the server code, confirming the store schema has no blob/image column, and checking that extraction results aren't persisted after handling. I want to confirm all AI outputs pass through zod validation too—extractDocument using RawExtraction, tool agent calls wrapped in checked(), and the rejection classifier similarly guarded.\n\nFor the disclaimer and provenance checks, I'm planning property tests that verify every rendered report scheme includes the disclaimer text, both the ReportStep and CoverSheet show it, and that counts of \"verified on\" and provisional badges match the actual number of checks in the data, including ones nested inside collapsed details elements.\n\nNow I'm checking whether checks could double-render — confirming external checks only appear once via CheckItem since the readiness banner just lists labels, not full items. I'm also noting the cover sheet currently omits source/verified-on info for provisional items and considering whether that needs to be added to satisfy the \"on every check\" requirement.\n\nFor the decoder, I'm planning `classifyRejection(text, deps)` to call chat/completions with a text-only prompt tagged \"classify-rejection-v1\", validate the response against a Zod schema for code and confidence, retry once on failure, and log a hash of the input text rather than the raw text. I'm weighing whether to extract a shared JSON-completion helper to avoid duplicating this logic, leaning toward a small private helper function instead of a broader refactor.\n\nI'm also sketching a pure `decodeResult` function that maps a classification into a view with status, code, confidence, fix steps, and bilingual ask prompts, using a 0.7 confidence threshold (checking whether thresholds.ts already defines this) so that unknown codes, low confidence, or AI errors all fall into a \"needs verification\" state.\n\nFor that state, I want to show the fixed bilingual \"what to ask at the office\" questions, plus, if a code was still guessed, a \"possible reason\" note with its steps marked as uncertain. I'm also considering capping input length at 2000 characters and wondering whether to add a deterministic keyword-based fallback classifier for when the AI service isn't available.\n\nNow I'm drafting those office-question templates in both English and Assamese, making sure to use the correct Assamese character, and planning the API decode endpoint structure.\n\nI'm sketching the `/decode` page architecture: a server component for metadata and a client `DecodeApp` component handling language toggle, textarea input, loading states, and a results card showing the verdict, steps, correction location, and questions to ask, plus a sample-input button for quick testing.\n\nI should also add a bilingual \"Got a rejection? Decode it\" link somewhere in the main KaagazApp flow, likely near the scheme step, pointing users to the new decode page. Finally I need a `demo:reset` npm script that runs a Node file resetting the store, being careful about importing TypeScript and handling path issues.\n\nFor the reset logic itself, I'll add a `clearAll()` method on the store that wipes transactions and returns a count, then have RuleService reconcile during sync by unregistering removed schemes -- verifying via a test that after clearing and syncing, published versions drop out of the registry.\n\nI'm also weighing whether to support an optional upper age limit (e.g., \"only if under 10 minutes\"), but since making AgeRange.max optional would ripple into evaluation messages, flow logic, and schema definitions, I'll defer that and just note it as future scope in progress.md rather than implementing now. I'll also quickly confirm route handlers access request headers in the standard way before moving on to check thresholds.ts.\n\nI don't think the ConfirmStep flow needs changes. For vitest, I want to confirm how JSX/TSX gets transformed without an explicit React plugin configured -- likely Vite's oxc-based transform picks up the `react-jsx` setting from tsconfig and handles it automatically, but I'll just test it directly rather than reason it out further.\n\nWith that settled, I'll move to implementing item one: adminAuth.\n\nI've laid out a four-part plan from NEXT.md: admin passcode protection, a guardrails audit, the decoder feature, and a demo:reset script. Starting now with the admin passcode work.\n\n"}}
