{"ts":"2026-10-10T11:32:59.935Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"reasoning","reasoning":"I'm planning out the admin authentication: creating a helper that checks the passcode against each admin route using a constant-time comparison of hashed values to avoid timing attacks, returning a 401 response when it fails.\n\nI also need to handle the UI side - when passcode protection is enabled, the server shouldn't leak history data into the initial HTML for unauthenticated visitors, so the client should fetch history only after the passcode is verified via sessionStorage. The admin page itself should show just a passcode box until unlocked, and I'll need unit and route-level tests covering the protected/unprotected cases.\n\nNow for guardrails testing, I need to verify Aadhaar masking works end-to-end: the schema rejects full 12-digit Aadhaar numbers, the UI input caps at 4 digits, and the cover sheet uses the masking function. I'm checking whether rendering a component with renderToStaticMarkup will work under the test setup given the tsx/jsx config.\n\nI also want to make sure logs never leak personal data, including from the classifyRejection flow, and I'm flagging a potential risk: the rejection message sent to the AI model could contain a full Aadhaar number embedded in free text, so I should redact any 12-digit sequences before sending it off.\n\nFor the no-images-stored requirement, I'm thinking a static source scan test is the practical route — checking that no file under src writes to disk (via writeFile, createWriteStream, etc.) except the DB storage module.\n\nI also want static guardrails ensuring model calls and AI SDK imports only live in ai.ts, with zod validation on all extraction and classification outputs, plus a property test confirming the disclaimer appears on every generated report regardless of scheme.\n\nI should extend the disclaimer to the decoder page too, since it's effectively a report. And I need to double check provenance requirements - every check should show \"verified on\" and provisional status, though the cover sheet currently only shows the provisional marker, so I may need to add source/verified-on info there too given print space constraints.\n\nI also want to strengthen the fuzzy-match property test: for all built-in schemes with name checks, verify that any name check marked \"ok\" always has match.level equal to \"same,\" broadening the existing pmKisan-only test. I confirmed Aadhaar never appears unmasked in the UI - ConfirmStep only takes last 4 digits and CoverSheet masks it properly. Now moving to review the decoder module itself.\n\nI'm defining a pure classification schema with rejection code and confidence score, plus a decodeOutcome function that turns a classification into a status (matched or needs_verification), including fix steps and bilingual questions to ask at the office. The confidence threshold is set at 0.7, and when verification is needed, I'll still surface the guessed code's possible reason alongside the office questions.\n\nI'm also adding text redaction for model input, a max character cap, and the classifyRejection function in ai.ts that calls the completions API with JSON output validated against a zod schema, including one retry and structured logging.\n\nI'll avoid refactoring the existing fetch loop in extractDocument, instead writing a small private helper reused for classifyRejection even if it duplicates some logic slightly. For AI unavailability, I'm deciding the server should fall back to a needs_verification outcome with an aiError flag so the UI still shows helpful \"ask at office\" guidance rather than just failing.\n\nI'll skip adding a deterministic keyword fallback since it's out of scope for the demo and the live AI path already covers the expected failure scenarios. I'm placing the handler logic in the existing api.ts pattern, wiring up the decode route and page accordingly.\n\nFor the page itself, I need a client component with a language toggle defaulting to Assamese, a textarea, submit button, loading state, and a result card, with all strings organized under a `decode` key in i18n for both languages (making sure the i18n test catches correct Assamese characters). I also want to surface a \"Got a rejection? Decode it\" link from the scheme step of the home flow pointing to `/decode`, plus write tests covering the core decoder logic, threshold behavior, redaction, and mocked AI classification responses.\n\nFor resetting demo data, I'm thinking through adding a `RuleStore.clearAll()` method that wraps the deletion in a transaction so only the store module touches the database directly, then writing a script to invoke it — though I need to work out how a plain `.mjs` script can use the TypeScript store module, possibly leveraging Node's built-in type stripping support.\n\nI'm realizing a .mjs script avoids the typecheck issue since it's not included in tsconfig, and Node 24's native type stripping lets it import the .ts store file directly without a build step. I'm confirming the store class fields use erasable modifiers like `private readonly`, so stripping should work cleanly.\n\nBut there's a deeper issue: the running dev server keeps an in-memory registry of published scheme versions that never unregisters entries, so even if I delete rows from the database directly, the home page would still list stale versions unless the server restarts or I add logic to reconcile the registry against what's actually published in the DB.\n\nI'm thinking syncRegistry should compute the expected set as built-ins union published rows, then reset and re-register whenever it detects extras that shouldn't be there anymore — tracking built-in keys separately so a resetToBuiltins() helper can clear and rebuild the registry cheaply on each sync.\n\nI'm worried that wiping the registry could break tests that call registerScheme directly without going through the DB—I should check whether apiCheck.test.ts registers schemes and then triggers syncRegistry, which would erase them unexpectedly. I also want to confirm that a published version can't collide with a builtin's id+version, which shouldn't happen since registerScheme rejects duplicates and the policy enforces version bumps.\n\nFor the demo reset, I'm planning to add a `clearAll()` method on RuleStore that deletes everything in a transaction and returns the count, since the store should be the only writer. The reset script will resolve the DB path the same way as defaultDbPath (via KAAGAZ_DB_PATH or the default data path), dynamically import the store, and call clearAll on it—if the DB file doesn't exist yet, RuleStore will just create an empty one, which is fine. Since the sample rules live in code rather than the database, there's nothing to reseed after clearing. I still need to add the demo:reset script entry to package.json.\n\nNow I'm thinking through module system quirks: running the script might trigger an ExperimentalWarning for type stripping on newer Node versions, which I could suppress with a flag, and I need to double check whether importing store.ts as ESM works correctly given package.json doesn't declare \"type\": \"module,\" since Node's type-stripping behavior depends on that setting and could otherwise misinterpret the file as CommonJS.\n\nFor testing, I'll cover clearAll and the syncRegistry reconciliation behavior — publishing a version, clearing, then verifying the registry resets correctly and scheme listings reflect the right state. The optional AgeRange.max feature seems too risky to implement quickly given its ripple effects across messages and views, so I'll skip it and log it under future scope in the progress notes instead.\n\nPolish items like 360px layout should already be fine, and loading states are covered in the decoder, so now I want to check how registerScheme is used across tests and the apiCheck test.\n\nI now have the full picture. Before writing code, I'll check whether any tests register schemes in memory and then call `syncRegistry()`, since that determines how safely the registry can reconcile with the DB for demo:reset.\n\n"}}
