{"ts":"2026-10-10T11:34:18.182Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"reasoning","reasoning":"Node can strip types and run store.ts directly since it only imports types from the alias path, which get erased at runtime. It worked, though there's warning noise I could suppress — maybe simpler to just write a small wrapper script instead.\n\nI'll add a `scripts/demo-reset.mjs` that imports store.ts and calls a new `RuleStore.reset()` method, since store is the sole DB writer. Resetting should clear all rows so built-in v1 rule packs are restored from code, so I'll add a `clearAll()` method on RuleStore plus a `resetDemo(path)` helper that opens the store, clears it, closes, and returns the row count — with a test that saves/publishes a proposal, resets, and verifies the store is empty with a fresh registry.\n\nBut I realize a running server keeps published versions in its in-memory registry even after a DB reset, since syncRegistry only adds entries, so the home page would still show stale schemes until restart. I need to add an `unregisterScheme(id, version)` function to the registry that refuses to remove built-ins, then update syncRegistry to compare the registered non-builtin versions against the DB's published set and unregister anything extra — exporting a `BUILTIN_SCHEMES` list from the schemes index to support this check.\n\nFor the admin passcode, I'm sketching an `adminAuth.ts` module with a guard function that checks an `ADMIN_PASSCODE` env var, compares it against a request header using a timing-safe hash comparison, and returns a 401 JSON response if it fails. I'll need to apply this guard across all four admin routes, adjusting handlers like the rules GET route to accept the request object so the check can run.\n\nI also realize the admin page currently renders scheme history server-side, which would leak protected data even with the passcode guard in place—so if protection is enabled, that history should be fetched client-side after passcode entry instead of baked into the initial HTML.\n\nI'm weighing whether reading sessionStorage directly in a lazy state initializer would cause a hydration mismatch, versus doing it in an effect and calling an async unlock function — trying to figure out if that still trips the lint rule against synchronous setState calls inside effects.\n\nI'll just write the decode logic and test it against lint. I'm sketching a pure decoder module with a confidence threshold of 0.7, a schema for raw classifications with code and confidence, and a function that turns a classification into a result object flagging whether manual verification is needed, along with fix steps and bilingual questions to ask at the office when the code is unknown or confidence is low.\n\nFor classifyRejection, I'm planning a text-only completion with a system prompt listing codes, validated via zod with one retry and a fallback error, logging only an input hash and never the raw text, capped at 4000 chars. The model supplies the code and confidence directly, and I'll derive each code's description from fixStepsFor's titleEn to avoid duplicating hint text. Now I'm sketching the `/api/decode` POST endpoint shape.\n\nOn error I'll return status 200 with a null decode result and an aiError flag, while invalid requests get a 400. For the page, I'm planning a `/decode` route with a server component handling metadata and a client `DecodeApp` component, bilingual with Assamese default, adding new i18n keys for both languages plus a home link pointing users to the decode feature from the main app.\n\nNow I'm thinking through guardrails tests to ensure Aadhaar numbers stay masked everywhere—checking the confirm step field length, the extracted document schema rejecting full 12-digit Aadhaar numbers, the API rejecting full Aadhaar inputs with a 400, and considering edge cases like a name field accidentally containing digits.\n\nI also realize pasted text sent to the decoder could contain a full Aadhaar or account number before it reaches the model, so I want to add a redaction step that masks digit runs of 9+ characters down to the last 4, with special handling for the 4-4-4 Aadhaar pattern.\n\nFor testing the CoverSheet print rendering, since there's no React testing library, I can use `react-dom/server`'s renderToStaticMarkup in the Vitest node environment, though I need to check whether JSX in a `.test.ts` file will transform properly given the current test include patterns and tsconfig JSX settings.\n\nI'd test CoverSheet, ReportStep, and CheckItem that way — verifying masked Aadhaar appears but the raw account number doesn't, and both language disclaimers are present. Separately, I should add a static check scanning source files for filesystem write APIs outside the store module, confirming no document images get persisted during extraction.\n\nFor storage guardrails, I'll scan citizen-facing code for localStorage/IndexedDB usage (excluding admin sessionStorage for passcodes), check the rule_versions schema has no blob/image column, and confirm /api/extract never returns image data. Then for AI output validation, I'll lean on existing extractDocument fallback tests, add similar ones for classifyRejection, and verify runToolAgent never lets raw agent text drive decisions directly — only validated tool calls matter.\n\nNext I'll cover the mandatory disclaimer footer: a property test confirming every scheme produces non-empty English/Assamese disclaimers matching the constant, plus rendering checks on ReportStep, CoverSheet, and the decoder page to ensure both disclaimers show up. Then for provenance, I want every rendered CheckItem across all built-in schemes to show a verifiedOn date and a \"Provisional rule\" tag exactly when applicable, with the cover sheet reflecting provisional status too, and the report schema enforcing provenance via zod on every check.\n\nI'm noticing the cover sheet doesn't actually surface verifiedOn, which might be a gap against the \"every check\" requirement — but adding a full source+date line there risks making the print summary too long, so I'm leaning toward keeping the cover sheet with just the provisional marker and noting in the log that full provenance detail lives in the on-screen report, while still considering a compact source line as a middle ground.\n\nOn testing, I'm checking whether the fuzzy-match guardrail (any non-exact name match never evaluates as ok) is already covered for pm-kisan and planning to extend that same property test to orunodoi and widow pension schemes.\n\nI'm also weighing an optional upper age limit feature but deciding it touches too many surfaces (messages, flow, rule diff view, agent prompt) for the time budget, so I'm skipping it and just noting it as a follow-up item.\n\nFor the smaller remaining tasks, I'm adding ADMIN_PASSCODE to the env example, updating the agent smoke script to send the admin passcode header when it's set, and confirming the route handler's header-reading approach doesn't need changes.\n\nNow moving to implementation, starting with the admin auth module.\n\nI've mapped out the T4 plan: admin passcode protection, a guardrails audit, a decoder module, and a demo:reset script, to be implemented in that order with a commit at the end. Starting now with the admin passcode work.\n\n"}}
