{"ts":"2026-10-10T11:36:56.877Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"reasoning","reasoning":"With item 1's 229 tests done, I'm moving to the guardrails audit, checking that Aadhaar numbers are masked consistently across the UI, print output, and logs—verifying the confirm step, cover sheet masking, and that logs only store hashes rather than raw values.\n\nI'm thinking through whether I can actually test React components here without jsdom—using renderToStaticMarkup from react-dom/server could work server-side in Vitest since the components aren't client-only in a way that blocks this. That would let me verify ReportStep shows the disclaimer, CheckItem displays verification status and provisional badges, and CoverSheet properly masks Aadhaar numbers while hiding account digits entirely.\n\nFor the Aadhaar safety guarantee, I'm realizing the strict validation in ExtractedDoc already rejects anything that isn't exactly 4 digits, so I should test that POSTing a full 12-digit Aadhaar to /api/check returns a 400 without leaking the digits in the response, and that adding an extra field like a full \"aadhaarNumber\" also gets rejected by strict schema validation. I also want to check flow.ts to see how ConfirmStep parses a typed-in Aadhaar down to last 4 digits, and confirm there's no filesystem write anywhere in the image processing pipeline that could persist raw images.\n\nI'm thinking a \"guardrails.test.ts\" file makes sense to scan source files for forbidden write APIs, confirm store.ts's schema has no image/blob columns, and verify extraction logs never include base64 image data — essentially a static scan plus a schema check.\n\nMoving to the third guarantee, I'm tracing how AI outputs get validated: extractDocument runs through a zod safeParse, tool inputs get re-checked before execution, and I need to confirm classifyRejection follows the same validated path.\n\nFor enforcing that ai.ts is the single gateway to the model, I'm planning a source scan asserting only ai.ts references the chat completions endpoint or constructs an Agent/SDK import, with existing fallback tests covering bad output cases.\n\nThen for the disclaimer requirement, I'm checking that ReportStep and CoverSheet both render the disclaimer text for every sample, and considering tightening the schema so evaluate's disclaimer field must exactly equal the canonical DISCLAIMER constant rather than just being any string.\n\nNext, for provenance and the provisional badge, I need every CheckItem across all three report sections (and across schemes with empty docs) to show the verified-on date, source title, and a provisional marker when applicable, with CoverSheet also surfacing the provisional flag — confirming this isn't required on BenefitCard since the rule only applies to checks. Then I'm looking at whether fuzzy scoring can ever produce an \"ok\" result, noting the existing property test in evaluate.test.ts and considering whether to extend similar coverage into the guardrails tests across all schemes.\n\nI'm now running through a checklist of smaller details: adding a broader property test asserting that any \"name\" check with an \"ok\" result implies the same match level, using fast-check over names across all three schemes; verifying the AiLogEntry structure for the agent; checking the new decoder message field; confirming the admin ProposalCard doesn't expose Aadhaar; making sure no stray console logs exist outside ai.ts; confirming CheckItem gracefully handles a null sourceUrl; and checking that the printed CoverSheet only shows the applicant name, not account numbers.\n\nI'm also confirming the Aadhaar display only shows the last four digits in ConfirmStep, which is the intended masking behavior, and checking that Report.disclaimerEn parses fine as a plain string on the client. The agent's commentary text shown in admin is just informational, not a decision input, so that seems fine too. Now I want to look at flow.ts to check how the Aadhaar value is parsed in ConfirmStep.\n\n229 tests pass and typecheck is clean, so item 1 is done. Moving to the guardrails audit—I'll first check how the UI handles Aadhaar input and whether Vitest can render the `.tsx` report components to static HTML to test what users actually see.\n\n"}}
